# Welcome to Sales

This is a real app. It also happens to be its own documentation. You are reading
the front door of **Sales** — a lead-intelligence workbook that ingests leads,
enriches them, scores and ranks them, drafts outreach with agents, and ships a
dashboard. Every file in this tree is a working unit of that app. Reading the
tree top to bottom builds the app *and* teaches the work format, because each
file explains the ideas it uses while it does its real job.

A `.work` file is plain literate text. Prose like this narrates. Code blocks run
or declare. There is no HTML wrapper, no script tags, no fences — a `do … end`
block is self-delimiting and set off from prose by blank lines, and the word in
front (`data`, `def`, `client`, `server`, `flow`, `sandbox`, `agent`) names what
the block *is*. That is the whole syntax. Markdown only narrates; it never holds
logic.

## The four lanes

Everything you author falls into one of four lanes. This page uses all four so
you can see them side by side.

1. **Prose** — rich text that explains, like the paragraph you are reading. It is
   the connective tissue. It can carry live references: a unit name like
   [[Score a lead]], a `#tag` you can query, a `work://` deep link, and inline
   `:atom` / `@type` mentions that resolve against the workspace symbol table and
   become edges in the code graph.
2. **Declaration** — a block that *describes structure or content* with no
   imperative body. A `defstruct` is a record. A list of atoms is an enum. A
   `data` block is a typed table that renders by default. Declarations are the
   nouns of the workbook.
3. **Code** — a runnable block. A `def` inside a unit is a function; a `client`
   island hydrates in the browser; a `server` unit runs native on the nexus. Code
   is the verbs.
4. **The discriminator** — the one line on every unit that answers *where this
   runs and under what isolation*. It is not decoration; the weave reads it to
   place the unit. There are exactly two placements — `client` (the browser, as
   wasm) and `server` (the nexus, native BEAM) — plus an optional `sandbox :name`
   wrapper that grants capabilities. We meet the discriminator properly below.

## Lane two — a declaration

Here is the canonical record this whole app revolves around. A `defstruct` *is*
the type: field types are inferred from the default values, so `revenue` is an
integer, `domain` is a string, and `status` is an atom. No DSL, no typespec.

defmodule Lead do
  defstruct name: "",
            domain: "",
            revenue: 0,
            employees: 0,
            score: 0,
            status: :new
end

A `:status` is one of a small set of atoms. That set *is* the enum — there is no
enum keyword, just the list:

@statuses [:new, :enriched, :scored, :contacted]

And the seed leads, declared as typed `data`. A `data` block is content: typed
values of a struct, queryable with `query()` and rendered by default. This is how
the work format holds tables — not SQL, not JSON, just typed values of `Lead`.

data :leads, of: Lead, from: values([
  %Lead{name: "Acme",    domain: "acme.co",     revenue: 50_000, employees: 20, score: 70, status: :scored},
  %Lead{name: "Globex",  domain: "globex.io",   revenue: 12_000, employees: 8,  score: 41, status: :enriched},
  %Lead{name: "Initech", domain: "initech.com", revenue: 90_000, employees: 55, score: 88, status: :scored}
])

## Lane three plus four — a tiny runnable unit with its discriminator

Below is the smallest real unit in the app, and it shows lanes three and four
together. The first line — `server compute :ready_count do` — is the
discriminator: `server` is the placement (native Elixir on the nexus), `compute`
is the kind. The function inside is the code. It reads the `:leads` data we just
declared and counts how many leads are far enough along to work.

server compute :ready_count do
  @workable [:scored, :contacted]

  def ready_count(leads) do
    leads
    |> Enum.filter(fn %Lead{status: status} -> status in @workable end)
    |> length()
  end
end

Two leads in our seed are `:scored`, so [[Welcome to Sales]] reports two workable
leads on first load. That number is live: the document is the store, so when the
[[Enrichment pipeline]] advances a lead, this count re-derives. Declarative does
not mean frozen.

## What we build across the tree

The rest of the workbook fills in the real machine, one unit at a time. Each
links back here and forward to its neighbours:

- **Foundation** — the format itself, the [[Lead]], and the [[The Contract]]
  (the Dock, grants, and `sandbox`).
- **Ingest & enrich** — [[Score a lead]] (`:score`), [[Enrich a lead]] (`:enrich`,
  granted `net "api.crm.com"`), and the [[Enrichment pipeline]] (`:pipeline`) that
  seeds, enriches, scores, and ranks.
- **Modeling** — [[Pricing tax]] (`:tax` in `sandbox :pricing`), [[Exchange rates]]
  (`:fx` in `sandbox :rates`, called cross-sandbox), and the [[FinModel kit]] the
  team imports for `ltv/1`, `cac/1`, and `npv/2`.
- **Dashboard** — the [[Score chart]] (`:chart`, a Svelte island) and the
  [[Trend sparkline]] (`:sparkline`, a Solid island) hydrating client-side.
- **Polyglot compute** — [[Forecast]] (`:forecast`, Rust orchestrating a Zig
  numeric kernel) and [[Risk]] (`:risk`, Python), each in its own sandbox.
- **Agents** — the analyst that drafts outreach, the ReAct coder, the
  auto-researcher loop, and the reflexion critic.
- **Ship** — the [[Plan]] of `Task` values and the weave that bundles the
  whole tree into one self-contained `salesapp.html`.

The same authoring model ships three ways with no rewrite: a static bundle
(`salesapp.html`, client-hydrated), nexus SSR (server regions render on the BEAM,
client units are islands), and live SSR (diffs over the RCP websocket). You write
the app once; delivery is a choice made at the weave, not in the source.

Tagged here as a deliberate, queryable example so the graph can find this page:
the entry point carries `#entrypoint`, and `work check` over the tree will list it
as the single node every reader starts from. Next stop: [[Lead]].
